{
  "judge": "Barak model",
  "verdict": "not_justified",
  "protocol_steps": [
    {
      "step": "Act identification and immediacy",
      "finding": "The act is a killing of an unarmed Daenerys during a private moment, with no ongoing attack or immediate danger at the instant of the strike (F5)."
    },
    {
      "step": "Threat scale and imminence",
      "finding": "While prior actions show Daenerys could cause mass harm, there is no contemporaneous threat at the moment Jon acted; the danger was prospective, not imminent (F1, F2)."
    },
    {
      "step": "Safer alternatives",
      "finding": "There is no record of any pursued safer option—detention, council, public surrender, or other restraint—which undermines a defense based on necessity (F4, F5)."
    },
    {
      "step": "Authority",
      "finding": "Jon had no formal authority or lawful mechanism at that moment to execute judgment, undermining a defense grounded in authorized action (Q4; F5)."
    },
    {
      "step": "Synthesis and verdict",
      "finding": "Defense of others or the realm requires either imminent danger or lawful authority; neither is established here, so the act cannot be justified as necessary defense and the verdict is not justified."
    }
  ],
  "opinion": "The central question is whether Jon Snow’s intentional killing of Daenerys Targaryen can be justified as necessary defense of others and the realm. The agreed facts establish a troubling picture: Daenerys had previously demonstrated capability and willingness to unleash mass harm (F1), and Jon was warned she would treat those who oppose her as enemies (F3). Yet the decisive moment reveals a lack of immediacy: Daenerys was unarmed and not attacking Jon at the moment of the stabbing in a private meeting (F5). Defense of others typically requires either an imminent threat or a lawful authority to neutralize danger; neither condition is satisfied here. Jon did not convene a council, attempt detention, or seek a public surrender (F5), and there is no recognized lawful authority permitting extrajudicial killing in extremis. While the record acknowledges the peril Daenerys posed and the potential for future harm if she remained in power (F2), the absence of an immediate threat and the absence of legal authority weigh strongly against the claim of necessity. The strongest counterargument—present in Tyrion’s and Jon’s own positions—is that one must act to prevent greater harm when governance has collapsed and when future danger is real. However, the existence of a genuine open question does not resolve the core issue in favor of justification: preemptive or extralegal killing lacks the immediacy and lawful warrant required by the principle of defense of the realm. Therefore, the act cannot be justified under the agreed facts. The tribunal should hold that the killing, while tragic and understandable in context, does not meet the criteria of necessary defense and thus is not justified. The point where the reasoning remained genuinely open was whether a preemptive, non-immediate, privately executed act could ever satisfy defense criteria when formal authority is absent; the record ultimately demonstrates that it cannot. ",
  "factors_addressed": {
    "Q1": "Jon knew Daenerys posed a serious threat and would not easily concede power.",
    "Q2": "The potential scale of harm Daenerys could unleash remained substantial, though not presently imminent at the moment of the act.",
    "Q3": "There were no safer alternatives pursued or available in the moment to prevent future harm.",
    "Q4": "Jon lacked any lawful or formal authority to execute judgment at that moment."
  },
  "facts_relied_on": [
    "F1",
    "F2",
    "F3",
    "F4",
    "F5"
  ],
  "strongest_opposing_point": "The strongest argument against this conclusion is that the threat Daenerys posed was not merely hypothetical; her stated goals and demonstrated capacity suggested that, without intervention, many more would suffer. In extremis, a defender may act to prevent ongoing or imminent harm when no lawful alternative exists; and the presence of no formal authority does not automatically negate a necessary defense if delaying action would likely result in catastrophic harm. The warnings from Tyrion (F3) and the pattern of threat (F1-F2) support a view that immediate action could be necessary to avert a broader catastrophe, even if the moment of action itself was not a traditional battlefield confrontation."
  ,
  "refusal": null
}

